<p>A gold loan turns idle family jewellery into emergency credit in under 30 minutes — faster than any other secured loan product. When you need ₹2 lakhs for a medical expense and cannot wait 3–5 days for a personal loan approval, pledging gold at a bank or NBFC is often the quickest path. The interest rate you pay depends on where you borrow: bank gold loan rates and NBFC gold loan rates differ meaningfully — always verify current rates with specific lenders before pledging. The loan amount you can receive depends on the current gold price and the lender's Loan-to-Value (LTV) ratio, which is subject to RBI-mandated LTV caps (verify the current LTV ceiling at rbi.org.in before pledging).</p>

<p><strong>Gold loan interest is not tax-deductible</strong> under any income tax provision for personal borrowing. Unlike a home loan — where Section 24(b) allows up to ₹2 lakh in annual interest deduction for self-occupied property under the old tax regime — gold loan interest provides zero tax benefit when the loan is used for personal purposes such as medical expenses, weddings, or consumption. If the gold loan funds a business or income-generating purpose, deductibility depends on the use of funds and requires advice from a chartered accountant. Gold pledged as collateral remains in the lender's physical custody until the loan is fully repaid; a missed EMI or bullet repayment triggers an auction process after legal notice to the borrower. Use the <a href="/india/personal-loan-emi-calculator/">Personal Loan EMI Calculator</a> to compare the cost of a personal loan alternative, and the <a href="/india/loan-prepayment-calculator/">Loan Prepayment Calculator</a> to model early repayment if you borrow at a high NBFC rate.</p>
